arch=arm64
vmlinux_btf=6.8 kernel BTF dump (bpftool btf dump file /sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux format c, Ubuntu aarch64, 6.8.0-139-generic)
clang=Ubuntu clang version 18.1.3 (1ubuntu1)
load_verified=bpftool prog loadall, Ubuntu aarch64 (6.8.0-139-generic), all 14 programs pinned
notes=rebuilt for P0 (mnt_want_write/write_mnt_map, resolve_dentry,
  dual renamedata layout, mount-boundary advance), the P2 ABI MINOR 2 contract
  (euid/login_uid) and the P3 LSM parity work (14 programs), from a real aarch64
  host's BTF.
  Its header is 6.8 while the x86 object's is 6.17; that is not an oversight but
  what each available host runs, and it does not affect correctness -- CO-RE
  resolves field offsets against the kernel the object is loaded on, not against
  the header it was compiled with. The 6.8 header is in fact the more useful of
  the two for the rename branch: its struct renamedata still has new_dir and no
  new_parent, so compiling against it exercises the bpf_core_field_exists()
  fallback path that the 6.17 header cannot reach.
